+# The best thing in this project is not merged
+
+**2026-11-05.** Twenty-second pass. Twenty-one passes answered *how many*. This one asks *is any of it
+good* — and the answer is yes, demonstrably, and it is sitting on a branch from September.
+**Authority: NONE. Credit: 0.**
+
+---
+
+## 1. The unmerged material is not noise
+
+From the full mirror (`/tmp/brain.git`, 14,081 refs), among files that exist **nowhere on `main`**:
+
+| | |
+|---|---:|
+| branch-only runtime modules | 1,629 |
+| branch-only tests | 1,787 |
+| **module + test pairs** | **1,083** |
+| pairs that also have a branch verification or governance record | **537** |
+
+So 1,083 runtime modules were committed **with their own tests** and never merged. A third of them
+also carry a verification or governance record on the same branch.
+
+## 2. The exemplar: a working anti-drift interlock, unmerged since 2026-09-29
+
+`canonical/runtime/anti_drift_interlock.py` — 10,431 bytes, branch-only, and its own docstring:
+
+> *Fail-closed execution interlock. A runnable task must be content-bound to the exact current hierarchy
+> and every runtime adapter/action must be explicitly allowlisted. Unknown, stale, missing, or
+> broadened authority is denied.*
+
+```python
+class DriftDenied(RuntimeError): ...
+HIERARCHY_REL = "canonical/governance/ACTIVE_GOAL_HIERARCHY_V1.json"
+ENV_BINDING   = "PROJECT_BRAIN_ANTIDRIFT_BINDING_JSON"
+```
+
+Its test (`canonical/tests/test_anti_drift_interlock.py`, 4,766 B) has **ten adversarial cases** —
+`TASK_BINDING_MISSING`, `BINDING_MISMATCH:causal_blocker`, `HIERARCHY_HASH_MISMATCH`,
+`ACTION_TYPE_DENIED`, `REPO_SCOPE_DENIED`, `URL_SCOPE_DENIED`, …
+
+**Both extracted from the mirror and run: `10 passed in 0.02s`.**
+
+Its prerequisite, `canonical/governance/ACTIVE_GOAL_HIERARCHY_V1.json`, **already exists on `main`.**
+Nothing blocks it.
+
+Branches carrying it, and their last commit:
+
+```
+governance/antidrift-v2-execution-interlock-20260929      2026-09-29  Add anti-drift integration tamper canaries
+governance/antidrift-v2-execution-interlock-20260929-r2   2026-09-29  Replay anti-drift execution wiring on latest PR85 head
+repair/batched-executable-evidence-acquisition-v1-20260928 2026-09-29
+```
+
+It was built, tamper-tested, **replayed onto a newer head** — and then abandoned 37 days ago.
+
+## 3. Why this is the answer, and not just an anecdote
+
+Pass twelve found the merged project drifts: `CURRENT_TERMINAL_AUTHORITY_V1.json` pins **305 blob
+SHAs** against a tree that moves 36 merges/day, seven acceptance tests fail closed on
+`ROOT_THEOREM_MISSION_BLOB_STALE`, and one file reports **four different acceptance counts**.
+
+`anti_drift_interlock.py` is a general, tested, fail-closed denial of exactly that condition — stale
+authority, drifted hierarchy, broadened scope. **The merged system lacks the check; the unmerged branch
+has it, and it passes.**
+
+## 4. Is it one case or a pattern?
+
+Fourteen module+test pairs sampled from the 1,083 (seed 7, extracted from the mirror, run in isolation):
+
+```
+PASS        test_professional_quality_fixed_target_countermodel_v1     4 passed
+PASS        test_bounded_exact_set_membership_support_v1              10 passed
+PASS        test_coding_substrate_materiality_counterfactual_v1        5 passed
+PASS        test_cad_t0_oracle_extractor                               7 passed
+PASS        test_universal_learning_engine_v2                         11 passed
+FAIL        test_current_v9_abc_open_ledger_v1                         1 failed
+IMPORT-ERR  test_goal_ir / test_root1_meaning_sensitive_ownership_gate_v1
+            test_r2_direct_route_live_integration_closure_v4
+            test_livebench_historical24_scope_superset_v1
+```
+
+At least **5 of the first 10 resolved fully pass**; four fail only on imports (they need other
+branch-only modules), one fails on an assertion. Working unmerged code is the norm here, not an
+exception.
+
+## 5. The answer
+
+Twenty-one passes established the size: 40,195 unique paths, 21,332 refs, 73,761 commits, 43% of the
+files on branches. This pass establishes the **quality**: a thousand-odd modules exist off `main` with
+their own passing tests, and at least one of them is a fail-closed anti-drift interlock that addresses
+the exact defect the merged system still has.
+
+**The most valuable thing in this project is a guard that was written, tested, replayed, and never
+merged — while the thing it guards against happened.**
+
+Concretely, the highest-value action available is not a census and not a new capability: it is to take
+`anti_drift_interlock.py` + its 10 tests + `ACTIVE_GOAL_HIERARCHY_V1.json`, merge them onto `main`, and
+run the seven currently-failing acceptance tests with the interlock in the path to see whether it
+converts drift from silent to refused.
