+# How much unmerged work actually runs — measured over all 1,083 pairs
+
+**2026-11-05.** Twenty-fourth pass. Pass 22 sampled 14 pairs. This runs **all of them**.
+**Authority: NONE. Credit: 0.**
+
+Method: `scripts/measure_unmerged_module_tests_v1.py` against the full mirror of
+`moxnixmdj/brain` — every branch-only runtime module that has a matching branch-only test is
+extracted **alone** into a clean tree and its test is executed. Isolating the pair strips its
+dependencies, so a `PASS` is a *lower bound* on functionality, never an overstatement.
+
+---
+
+## 1. The result
+
+```
+branch-only modules   1,629
+branch-only tests     1,787
+module+test pairs     1,083
+wall clock            320 s
+```
+
+| outcome | count | share |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| **PASS** — runs green alone | **319** | 29.5% |
+| IMPORT_ERROR — needs other branch files | 583 | 53.8% |
+| FAIL — ran and failed its own test | 179 | 16.5% |
+| OTHER | 2 | 0.2% |
+
+**At least 319 runtime modules exist only on branches and pass their own tests in isolation.**
+`main` has 2,335 runtime modules in total, of which exactly **27** capabilities are measured working
+end to end. The branch store holds a further 319 that are verifiably functional and 583 more that are
+plausibly functional but unmeasurable in isolation.
+
+179 pairs are unmerged **and** failing their own tests — abandoned work that would not have passed.
+
+## 2. Forty-two of the passing modules are checkers
+
+Filtering the 319 for names concerning drift, claims, receipts, audits, staleness and enforcement
+yields **42**, including:
+
+```
+canonical/runtime/anti_drift_interlock.py
+canonical/runtime/mission_semantic_authority_v1.py
+canonical/runtime/atomic_one_use_execution_claim_v1.py
+canonical/runtime/bound_executor_wrapper_nonwidening_audit_v1.py
+canonical/runtime/executable_refinement_receipt_guard_v1.py
+canonical/runtime/acceptance_receipt_saturator_v1.py
+canonical/runtime/chartography_brain_credit_subject_gate_v2.py
+canonical/runtime/p1_terminal_archive_sufficiency_audit_v1.py
+canonical/runtime/h100_registry_causal_provenance_audit_v1.py
+```
+
+## 3. The two that answer the defect this session found
+
+Pass twelve measured the project's central pathology: `CURRENT_TERMINAL_AUTHORITY_V1.json` pins
+**305 blob SHAs** against a tree that moves 36 merges/day, seven acceptance tests fail closed on
+`ROOT_THEOREM_MISSION_BLOB_STALE`, and one file carries four different acceptance counts.
+
+Both fixes exist on branches, tested, unmerged:
+
+**(a) `anti_drift_interlock.py`** — 10,431 B, fail-closed. Denies stale/missing/broadened authority at
+admission. Verified in pass 23: 10/10 tests, complete with its policy block and wiring on
+`governance/antidrift-v2-execution-interlock-20260929` — 16/16 green on the branch — and inert against
+`main` until `main` adopts the policy block it enforces.
+
+**(b) `mission_semantic_authority_v1.py`** — 2,602 B, passing. Instead of pinning bytes it hashes the
+mission's **semantics**:
+
+```python
+SEMANTIC_FIELDS = ("real_objective", "optimization_objective", "compounding_requirement",
+                   "knowledge_policy", "opus_5_5_role", "benchmark_role", "checkpoint_rule",
+                   "correctness_rule", "spend_rule", "mission_terminality",
+                   "option_preservation_requirement", "future_technology_compatibility")
+```
+
+That is exactly the fix recommended in `ACCEPTANCE_DRIFT_20261105_V1.md` §5 — bind acceptance to a
+semantic identity that does not change when unrelated files move, instead of to 305 blob SHAs.
+
+## 4. The answer
+
