+# What Project Brain actually has — every real and valuable thing, and what each is worth
+
+**2026-11-05.** Written by an incoming agent asked to join the project and report *what is real*
+and *how valuable it is*, seeing everything important or valuable.
+
+**Authority: NONE.** This is an inventory and a valuation, not a receipt and not a capability
+claim. `truth.achieved` stays `false`. Nothing here flips a family, promotes a capability, or
+grants credit. Every number below was produced on this machine today, or is quoted from a
+document in the tree and labelled with its date. Where I say "I ran it", I ran it.
+
+The one rule I applied throughout: **a thing is real only if it executes and produces a verified
+effect.** A file that describes work is not work. A registry entry is not a capability. So the
+tiers below are ranked by *measured execution*, not by how impressive a document sounds.
+
+---
+
+## 0. How I verified (so you can re-run every line)
+
+| What I ran | Result |
+|---|---|
+| `execution_guard/agent_orientation_check_v1.py brief` / `verify` | CONSISTENT; prints the objective, laws, live state |
+| `execution_guard/repo_integrity_guard_v1.py` | `mode=static+diff rules=17 violations=0 -> PASS` |
+| Loaded all 170 bound-capability adapters | **159 import cleanly, 143 expose `run()`; 11 fail, 9 of them only for missing third-party deps** |
+| `goal_compiler.compile_goal()` on 5 plain-English goals | 3 correct selections, 1 fail-closed on a goal it cannot bind, 1 correct fail-closed refusal on nonsense |
+| `brain_unified_model_v1 status / route / recall / solve` | works; `solve` returned `PASS__PROMOTED_BOUND_CAPABILITY_EXECUTED_AND_TRACE_VERIFIED` |
+| `compile_brain_unified_model_v1.py` then byte-compare | **byte-identical recompile confirmed** |
+| `true_capability_harness_v1.py run` on 3 ledger specs, fresh `OS_URANDOM` seeds | **500/500, 500/500, 500/500 pass** across 4/2/2 independent oracle classes |
+| `pytest` on the crown-jewel slice (6 files) | 115 passed + 911 subtests, **3 failed** |
+| `pytest` on the creature slice (7 files) | **74 passed, 0 failed** |
+| `pytest --collect-only` over `canonical/tests` | **16,976 tests collected, 42 collection errors** |
+
+Machine shape: **16,311 tracked files, 151,510 lines of Python, 2,840 non-test modules, 2,439 test
+files, 334 CI workflows, 9,866 JSON records under `canonical/`, 136 root-level dated reports.**
+
+---
+
+## 1. The one-paragraph verdict
+
+This is not a project with nothing, and it is not the project its own names suggest. What is
+**genuinely real** is a narrow, unusually honest core: a truth layer that actually refuses credit,
+a deterministic plain-English→verified-execution solver that actually runs, a 443-entry bound
+capability surface with a single front door, nine capability kernels that reproduce PASS on fresh
+random instances, a process that survives being killed, and a creature assembly whose organs
+compose. What is **not real** is the headline: 15 of 19 capability families route through an
+external model, so under the project's own law they carry **zero credit**; the ownership matrix
+says 7/19 owned and the audit says those 7 are not owned in the sense the law requires. The
+single most valuable asset here is not a capability at all — it is the **machinery that prevents
+you from lying to yourself**, and it works.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Tier 1 — Real, running, and valuable
+
