+## 5. Defects I found today (real, reproducible, none of them previously recorded)
+
+### 5.1 The README's own example command fails
+The documented `solve` example uses `"output_path":"/tmp/audit.json"`. Running it verbatim returns
+`RuntimeError:PATH_OUTSIDE_REPOSITORY` / `FAIL_CLOSED`. With a repo-local path it returns
+`PASS__PROMOTED_BOUND_CAPABILITY_EXECUTED_AND_TRACE_VERIFIED`. **The first command a newcomer runs
+fails.** One-word documentation fix, high annoyance cost.
+
+### 5.2 The unified-model README is stale against its artifact
+Documented: 364 capabilities, digest `d050fac6…`, "7 dependencies missing, 357 present".
+Live: **443 capabilities, digest `9904fa7c…`, 33 missing, 410 present.** A reader cannot tell
+whether the digest mismatch means the artifact was tampered with or the doc rotted. In this
+repository that ambiguity is expensive, because digest mismatch is exactly the tamper signal.
+
+### 5.3 A live gate is red and a capability fails closed
+- `test_live_capability_invoker_v1::test_catalog_proves_universal_invocation_contract_coverage`
+  fails because `r1_live_architecture_closure_gate` returns
+  `FAIL_CLOSED__R1_LIVE_ARCHITECTURE_DRIFT` with five errors —
+  `AUTHORITY_FABRIC/INVOKER/SUBRUNTIME_BLOB_DRIFT` and two `INVOCATION_AUTHORITY_*_BLOB_DRIFT`.
+  **The pinned blob SHAs of `unified_cognitive_fabric_v1`, `live_capability_invoker_v1` and
+  `live_brain_runtime_v1` do not match the files on disk.** Either the pins are stale or the
+  authority files were edited without re-pinning — both are governance events, and the gate is
+  correctly refusing.
+- `test_verified_bound_capability_execution_adapter_v1` fails closed on
+  `aa_briefcase_component_summary_noninversion_v1.py.auto.v1`.
+- `test_bound_capabilities::test_python_stdlib_capabilities_declare_importable_module` fails on
+  `python.review.diff_coverage_gate.v1` (declared as a stdlib module; it is not importable).
+
+### 5.4 The frozen credited supervisor still carries a known defect
+`same_identity_supervisor_credited_v1.py` carries the missing terminal-state guard that was fixed
+in the live copy on 2026-10-14. A frozen copy is supposed to be evidence of what was credited — so
+this is arguably correct-but-alarming: the credited evidence has a defect the live code does not.
+
+### 5.5 Two registry entries point at an adapter that does not exist
+`knowledge.consistency.assess` and `knowledge.json.fetch.external` both declare
+`adapter_module: runtime_native_action`; **no `runtime_native_action.py` exists** in
+`bound_capabilities/`. 139/141 registry paths resolve; these two do not.
+
+### 5.6 Nine adapters fail on missing third-party deps, one on a packaging quirk
+9 of 11 import failures are `PIL` (7 chart adapters + `chartography` +
+`professional_artifact_visual_observer`), 1 is `sympy`. The 11th,
+`knowledge_status_adapter`, imports its sibling `source_candidate_provenance_verify` by **bare
+name** — it works when its directory is on `sys.path` (I verified) and fails otherwise, which makes
+it order-dependent under any loader that does not add the directory.
+
+### 5.7 ~98 MB of committed scratch — 30% of the repository
+`canonical/astra_runtime/tmp/` **56.4 MB in 464 tracked files** (including a 5.0 MB `lxml` wheel and
+several 2–4 MB PNGs) and `canonical/astra_runtime/state/` **41.3 MB in 560 tracked files**
+(including 1.6 MB controller checkpoints). Against a 321 MB tree, that is roughly **98 MB of run
+artifacts** versioned as canon. It slows every clone, obscures the real evidence, and nothing
+distinguishes a saved scratch file from a receipt.
+
+### 5.8 Registry-to-live-count drift is confusing by construction
+Three different numbers are all "the capabilities": **141** (the JSON registry file), **443** (the
+unified model's bound set), **490** (live owned = 443 + 25 engines + 21 H100 + 1 certified route).
+All three are correct for their own definition, and the README picks none of them consistently.
+Any newcomer will report the wrong number. State one canonical definition and derive the others.
