+# V3 — the answer, with the number that was missing from V1 and V2
+
+**2026-11-05.** Supersedes V1 and V2. V2 was an exhaustive *inventory*. It was still not an answer,
+because it never measured the one thing that decides value: **what the live solver actually does
+end to end on goals it has never seen.** Now it does.
+
+**Authority: NONE.** `truth.achieved` stays `false`. No credit is claimed or granted here.
+
+---
+
+## 1. The honest answer to "are you sure this is all of them?"
+
+**No — and I can now say precisely what was missing rather than promising completeness again.**
+
+Twice I swept inventory (counts, titles, docstrings) and declared it complete. Both times I had
+skipped the **content** of the biggest record classes and, worse, I never ran the project's own
+end-to-end instrument. What V3 adds:
+
+| Never done in V1/V2 | What it produced |
+|---|---|
+| Read `CURRENT_TERMINAL_AUTHORITY_V1.json` | the live authority: 7/19, 18/38, `achieved: false`, plus **3,352/3,352 raw executions pass — immutable** |
+| Aggregated all **780 action intents** | the actual history of claimed progress: **24 capability-gain claims vs 62 falsifications** |
+| Extracted all **16,976 test names** | the real promised surface, and the biggest test file (**345 cases**) I had never opened |
+| Ran the **live solver end-to-end instrument** | **47.22% in-scope instance pass rate** — §3 |
+| Read the **computer-execution boundary** | the gate that blocks the owner's actual goal — §4.1 |
+| Mapped **version families** | 88 `osworld` modules, 12 `universal_verified_adaptive_solver`, 21 `bytes` |
+| Characterised the **20 largest modules** | `raw_goal_trace_acceptance`, `verifier_falsification`, `value_algebra` — §5 |
+
+What I still have **not** read line by line: the full prose of all 136 root documents, all 2,163
+module bodies, all 9,866 `canonical/*.json` records. I have now measured or opened a representative
+of **every class**, and I have run the two instruments that produce the decisive numbers. That is the
+strongest claim I can honestly make, and it is a judgment about coverage, not a proof of totality.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Why "every valuable thing" is the wrong frame — and what to ask instead
+
+This repository has ~20,000 files and perhaps **six** things that carry value. The other 19,994 are
+either evidence *of* those six, history *of* attempts at them, or scaffolding that verifies them.
+Asking "is this all of them" invites an infinite regress — I could always find a seventh document.
+
+The question that has an answer is: **what is irreducible?** Delete these and nothing else works.
+That set is small, and after three passes I am confident it is complete, because each item is
+load-bearing for a *measured* number rather than for a document.
+
+---
+
+## 3. The number that decides everything: 47.22%
+
+I ran `execution_guard/live_solver_end_to_end_measurement_v1.py` — the project's own honest
+instrument, written to measure "plain goal → compiler → planner → controller → bound tool →
+artifact" through the public entry points, with an independent stdlib oracle reading the real
+artifact, on goals it has never seen. Fresh `OS_URANDOM` seed `435920808002669906`, `$0`,
+`model_dependency_count: 0`, `authority: NONE`.
+
+```
+run_complete: True    37 families measured, 5 fresh instances each
+
+                    OLD entry (live_integrated_brain.run_raw_goal)
+                    NEW entry (live_brain_route_admission.solve)
+in-scope instance pass rate      OLD 36.67%     NEW 47.22%
+families fully passing          OLD 13/37      NEW 14/37
+families not fully passing       OLD 24         NEW 23
+```
+
+Families that pass **completely** on the new front door: archive creation from manifest, archive
+verify, compound two-step literal JSON, exact literal response, literal JSON records, SQLite, source
+ranking (BM25), receipt-prose execution, and a handful of others.
+
+Families that score **0/5** on a fresh run include: `CHART_RASTER_READERS`, `CODEC_CBOR`,
+`CODEC_MSGPACK`, `DOCX_CREATE`, `PDF_TEXT_EXTRACT`, `NUMERIC_EXPRESSION`, `PYTHON_AST_PATCH_SEARCH`,
+`PYTHON_REPAIR_COUPLED_AST`, `PYTHON_REPAIR_COUPLED_PROV`, `PIPELINE_FETCH_THEN_AUDIT`.
+
+**This is the single most important number in the project**, and it was not in V1 or V2. It is the
+difference between *reachability* and *capability*:
+
+- **490** identities are addressable from one front door (`REACHED_AND_BOUND: 419`,
+  `REACHED_BY_EXPLICIT_NAME: 19`, `NOT_REACHABLE: 2`, `ROUTED_ELSEWHERE: 3`). **Reachability: ~99%.**
+- **47.22%** of fresh in-scope instances are actually solved. **Capability: ~47%.**
+
+Anything that says "443 capabilities" is describing the first number. The second is the product.
+
+**And 47% is a floor, not the ceiling, in this sandbox.** The same run reports
+`adapter_import_failed: 9`, `external_dependency_missing_here: 9`, and 19 capabilities with a
+declared zero-input surface. The zeros above are largely `PIL` (9 chart/visual adapters), `sympy`,
+`msgpack`/`cbor2`/`bson`, `tesseract`/`pypdf`, and `jq` metadata — all absent on this host. So a
+material share of the missing 53% is **unmet substrate, not broken logic.** The instrument is
+scrupulous about this: it calls these environment blockers and refuses to count a missing module as a
+capability failure. That distinction is one of the best pieces of engineering in the tree.
+
+---
+
+## 4. The two gates that actually stand between here and the owner's goal
+
+The owner's goal has two halves, and each is blocked by exactly one verified gate. Both are located,
+both are documented, and I verified both today.
+
+### 4.1 "It can do anything on the computer by itself" → **blocked on confinement, not on capability**
+
+The largest test file in the repository — `test_an_ordinary_sentence_runs_the_command_it_states_v1.py`,
+**345 cases, 290 KB** — tests exactly the owner's demand: an ordinary sentence states a command in
+words, and the machine runs it and is checked against the real filesystem. I ran it:
+
+```
+233 failed, 112 passed
+```
+
+Every failure is one refusal:
+
+```
+FAIL_CLOSED__COMPUTER_EXECUTION_REQUIRES_ADMITTED_CONFINEMENT
+```
+
+That is not a bug. It is a deliberate, documented boundary
+(`canonical/runtime/computer_execution_boundary_v1.py`, and
+`COMPUTER_EXECUTION_BOUNDARY_DRAFT_20261102.md`): the machine will not execute arbitrary programs
+until a genuinely confined carrier is admitted, and it fails closed rather than pretending.
+
+**I verified the host facts myself rather than trusting the document:**
+
+```
+unshare     /usr/bin/unshare      → unshare -Ur WORKS
+cgroup      /sys/fs/cgroup        → NOT writable (dr-xr-xr-x root root)
+docker / podman / nsjail / bwrap / firejail / proot  → ALL MISSING
+```
+
+So the refusal is *correct on this host*. There is no aggregate-resource-enforced carrier available.
+The path to the owner's goal is therefore not a capability problem at all — it is a
+**substrate-provisioning problem**: a pinned minimal image, delegated cgroups, and a bounded broker
+with independent artifact validation. `test_computer_unconfined_route_guard_v1` +
+`test_a_precise_refusal_is_not_replaced_by_a_generic_one_v1`: **18 passed**.
+
+### 4.2 "It gets smarter from experience" → **blocked on one predicate**
+
+Unchanged from V2 and still open, verified in live code at
+`canonical/swe_kernel/meta_circular_organ_graph_v1.py:3953`:
+
+```python
+if not (len(fn.body) == 1 and isinstance(fn.body[0], ast.Return) and fn.body[0].value is not None):
+    return None     # rejects every program the synthesizer emits (Assign, Assign, Return)
+```
+
+The concept library `G_t` therefore never grows, so nothing compounds. The 11-04 note's own
+pre-registered falsifier already exists: *a period-12 structure, never seen, must cost strictly fewer
+oracle questions after periods 3/5/7 are compressed than cold.* A cache cannot pass it.
+
+**These are the only two things that matter.** One is a provisioning task. One is a one-function
+change with a test already written.
+
+---
+
+## 5. What is genuinely valuable — the irreducible set, ranked
+
+| # | Irreducible asset | Why it is irreducible | Value |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | **`live_solver_end_to_end_measurement_v1.py`** + the discipline it enforces | The only thing in 20,000 files that produces the 47.22% number. Without it every claim is reachability cosplay | ★★★★★ |
+| 2 | **`raw_goal_trace_acceptance_v1.py`** (161 K) — *"every claimed effect is re-opened from disk"* | The execution-level anti-lying mechanism: it never trusts `pass`/`status`, it re-reads the artifact. This is why 47% is believable | ★★★★★ |
+| 3 | **`verifier_falsification_v1.py`** (74 K) — *"prove that each verifier can say no"* | Meta-verification. Every verifier must ship a known-failing input, else it is untested. This is the highest-order idea in the repo | ★★★★★ |
+| 4 | **The referee + 780-intent ledger** (17 rules, 0 violations) | 780 intents record 190 integrations / 105 reliability gains / 62 falsifications / **24** capability gains. The project's own books say it has mostly *contracted* claims, not expanded ability | ★★★★★ |
+| 5 | **578 external public-runner verifications** (separate public repo, 2 recorded failures, zero credit) | The only evidence a stranger can check | ★★★★★ |
+| 6 | **The pre-registration corpus** (17 preregs + rounds 13–22) | Claims written before measurement, then kept when falsified | ★★★★★ |
+| 7 | **`value_algebra_v1.py`** (103 K) — *"answers are BUILT OUT OF typed pieces, not looked up in a menu"* | The composition thesis in code; the alternative to the fixed-menu defect the 11-04 rounds kept finding | ★★★★☆ |
+| 8 | **`live_brain_route_admission_v1.py`** (369 K, 43 refs) | The fail-closed front door that moved 36.67% → 47.22% | ★★★★☆ |
+| 9 | **`declared_input_derivation_v1.py`** (135 K) | Binds a human sentence to a typed capability contract — the hardest sub-problem | ★★★★☆ |
+| 10 | **True-capability harness + 9 kernels** | Reproduced 3/9 by me at 500/500 on seeds nobody chose | ★★★★☆ |
+| 11 | **`BRAIN_UNIFIED_MODEL_V1`** + byte-identical recompile | 443 identities behind one deterministic door | ★★★★☆ |
+| 12 | **Creature assembly** (74/74) | Seven organs in one body; the only organism | ★★★★☆ |
+| 13 | **`requirements/software_engineering_kernel_v1.txt`** | Hash-pinned z3/crosshair/hypothesis/tree-sitter — makes the SWE kernel reproducible | ★★★★☆ |
+| 14 | **`general_adequate_decision_controller_v1`** (88 K) | The "is this decision good enough" layer | ★★★☆☆ |
+| 15 | **`swe_observational_program_synthesizer_v1`** (91 K) | Deterministic synthesis + package builder | ★★★☆☆ |
+| 16 | **1,104 `astra_runtime` mission records** | Real execution receipts incl. honest false-complete corrections | ★★★☆☆ |
+| 17 | **24 named regression post-mortems** | A taxonomy of how this project fooled itself | ★★★☆☆ |
+| 18 | 18 internalized skills | Good content, **not wired** (`ACTIVE_VERIFIED_LEARNED_SKILL: 0`) | ★★☆☆☆ |
+| 19 | 6,697-record research index | Correctly never counted as capability | ★★☆☆☆ |
+
+**The three things V2 ranked below the referee but which are actually its peers** are #1–#3. V2 put
+the referee first and never noticed `raw_goal_trace_acceptance` and `verifier_falsification` — the two
+modules that make every other number honest at the execution and verification layers respectively.
+
+---
+
+## 6. New defects found in this pass
+
+| # | Defect | Severity |
+|---|---|---|
+| 6.1 | **The terminal authority carries four different acceptance numbers in one file**: status says `7_OF_19 … 18_OF_38`; an embedded `rule` string says `CURRENT_ACCEPTANCE_IS_5_OF_19_AND_12_OF_38`; another says `HISTORICAL_ACCEPTANCE_3_OF_19_8_OF_38_SUPERSEDED`; a 10-02 record says `2_OF_19_AND_7_OF_38`. All dated, none marked superseded at the point of use. **Which number an agent quotes depends on which line it reads** | **High** |
+| 6.2 | **The biggest and most mission-relevant test file is 67% red** (233/345) and nobody had surfaced it as the #1 test cluster — it is buried in a "789 real failures" total that hides its significance | **High** |
+| 6.3 | **88 `osworld*` modules** and 12 `universal_verified_adaptive_solver*` versions with no recorded supersession map in `runtime/` (unlike `laws/`, which has a strict rule) | Medium |
+| 6.4 | **19 capabilities declare a zero-input surface** (`supercomposer` family etc.) — addressable, unroutable from any sentence | Medium |
+| 6.5 | 425 of 2,163 modules have no docstring; ~1,900 are unreachable from any registry entry | Medium |
+| 6.6 | V1's six defects + V2's five all still stand (7 identical `.v1` files, 6 zero-byte `gz*`, OPUS_5_5 status-vs-truth contradiction, README staleness, failing README example, 5 BLOB_DRIFT errors, 98 MB scratch, 3 incompatible capability counts) | — |
+
+---
+
+## 7. What to do, in the only order that makes sense
+
+1. **Publish 47.22% as the headline.** Not 443, not 490, not "7/19 owned". One number, one seed, one
+   instrument, reproducible by anyone. Everything else is a leading indicator of it.
+2. **Fix `:3953`.** One predicate, one already-written falsifier. It is the difference between a
+   skill cache and a creature.
+3. **Provision confinement.** The owner's actual sentence — "do anything on the computer by itself" —
+   is gated on a pinned image, delegated cgroups, and a bounded broker. This is the largest single
+   capability unlock available and it is not a research problem.
+4. **Reconcile the four acceptance numbers** in the terminal authority to one derivation.
+5. **Install the 9 missing dependencies** and re-run the instrument. If the pass rate does not move,
+   that is itself the finding.
+6. Wire the 18 skills; clear the 5 BLOB_DRIFT errors; purge 98 MB of scratch; fix the two stale
+   README numbers.
+
+---
+
+## 8. Final answer to the question
+
+You have **six irreducible things**: an end-to-end instrument that tells the truth (47.22%), an
+acceptance layer that re-opens every claimed effect from disk, a falsifier that proves each verifier
+can say no, a referee with a 780-intent ledger whose own books show 24 gains against 62
+falsifications, 578 external verifications in a separate public repository, and a pre-registration
+corpus that keeps its own failed predictions. Around them sit 443 bound capabilities (99% reachable,
+47% solving), nine reproducible kernels, a creature that composes, and 1,104 mission receipts.
+
+You have **two gates**: confinement (a provisioning task) and compression (one predicate).
+
+And I am **not** sure I have listed every valuable thing — I am sure I have now measured the value of
+the system, which is the question underneath the question. The residual risk is not that a seventh
+treasure exists; it is that 47.22% is measured on a sandbox missing nine dependencies, so the true
+figure on a provisioned host is unknown and probably higher. That is the next measurement, not the
+next document.
